.TH CCOL_CHANNEL_DESTROY 3 "2026-09-26" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_channel_destroy \- destroy a ccol_channel
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_channel_destroy(ch);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Frees everything the ccol_channel holds, both of its queues included,
and then sets the ccol_channel pointer to NULL.

ccol_event_loop_remove() doesn't wait for a callback that it has
already handed to a reactor thread, so such a callback can still be
working inside one of the channel's queues when the removal returns.
This call blocks until every one of those callbacks is done with the
queue and only then frees it, which is what makes the documented
teardown order (drain, remove, destroy) safe as written.

A callback that the loop runs for a different registration can run that
whole sequence for this channel, with any num_reactor_threads. A
dispatch that the loop collected for the removed registration but did
not start yet doesn't hold the destroy up, and it never runs its
callback after the removal.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
ch   Channel to destroy. The macro sets it to NULL.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
You can call this with a NULL pointer.

ch is evaluated exactly once and must be a modifiable lvalue, such as
a variable or an array element. An expression with a side effect, such
as handles[i++], therefore destroys and clears a single element.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
A message left in either direction causes an assertion failure, so
drain both queues before you destroy the channel.

A watcher on either direction causes an assertion failure too. A
watcher is a ccol_select() call, a ccol_select_timed() call or a
ccol_event_loop registration (ccol_selectable_from_chan). Without the
assertion the waiter would stay linked and keep pointing at the mutex
of a queue that the destroy is about to free. Call
ccol_event_loop_remove() first, or let every ccol_select() and
ccol_select_timed() call that watches the channel return.

Do not call this from inside a ccol_event_loop callback that dispatches
one of the directions of this ccol_channel, because the destroy would
wait for that very callback. It is a caller error, and the program
stops through _ccol_fatal_err().

Do not hold an application lock across this call when one of those
callbacks takes the same lock: the destroy waits for the callback while
the callback waits for the lock.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_channel_create (3)
